        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many lumens do I need in an emergency lantern?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Around 100 lumens is enough for a small bedroom or bathroom, and 200 to 300 lumens comfortably lights a living room or kitchen. Higher settings are for short tasks. A lantern rated at 400 to 600 lumens gives you headroom to run it at a lower, longer-lasting setting most of the time, which is where its runtime claims apply."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a rechargeable lantern or a battery lantern better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Each has a role. Rechargeable lanterns are brighter, cheaper to run and often charge phones, but they must be charged before the outage. Battery lanterns that take D, AA or AAA cells can sit for years and are refilled in seconds. The most resilient setup is one rechargeable lantern for nightly use plus a battery lantern as backup."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do LED lanterns last on batteries?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on brightness, battery size and how the maker measured. Runtime claims are usually on the lowest setting and, under the ANSI FL1 standard, end when output falls to 10 percent of the start. Expect hours on high and days on low. D-cell lanterns generally outlast AA and AAA lights at the same brightness."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are hand-crank lanterns worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "As a backup feature, yes; as a main power source, no. A minute of cranking typically gives only a few minutes of moderate light, because hand generators produce very little power. A crank is valuable when every battery is dead and you need to find your way. Choose a lantern for its battery and dimmer first, and treat the crank as insurance."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it safe to use a camping lantern indoors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Battery-powered LED camping lanterns are safe indoors and are the best choice. Fuel-burning camping lanterns that use propane, white gas or kerosene are not, because they produce carbon monoxide, which can build up in a closed home. Keep fuel lanterns outdoors, and if any fuel appliance runs during an outage, have battery-powered CO alarms on every level."
          }
