        <div id="backups" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Hand-Crank, Solar and USB Output: Useful or Gimmick?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hand cranks are on a lot of emergency lights, and expectations need to be set honestly. Cranking a small generator by hand produces only a few watts at best, and a minute of steady cranking typically buys a few minutes of light at a moderate setting. That is not a way to run a lantern through a night. It is a way to get yourself out of a dark room when the battery is flat and nothing else is available. Treat a crank as a seatbelt, not an engine, and pick a lantern for its battery and dimmer first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Built-in solar panels work on the same principle at a slower pace. A small panel on top of a lantern like the LuminAID can recharge it over a day of direct sun, which is genuinely useful after a hurricane or ice storm that leaves the grid down for a week. It is far less useful through a window, under clouds, or in winter when the sun is low. For anything more than a single small lantern, a separate folding panel charging a power bank or power station is much more effective, and our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels</a> covers what size actually makes a difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            USB output, the ability to charge a phone from the lantern's battery, is the most useful of the three extras. A lantern with a several-thousand-mAh battery inside can add a meaningful partial charge to a phone at a pinch. The catch is that the lantern and phone now share one battery. Charge a phone from the lantern on the first night and you may lose a night of light. For phones, a dedicated power bank is the better tool, and our guide to <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">portable chargers for power outages</a> shows how much capacity a household really needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other direction matters as well: how does the lantern itself recharge? A lantern that charges over USB can be refilled from almost anything, including a car, a laptop, a power bank, or a <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station</a>. That flexibility is worth more than any built-in gimmick. Favor lanterns that charge over USB-C, which is now the standard and accepts the same cable as most current phones, and avoid anything that charges only through a proprietary adapter you will not be able to find in the dark.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="house-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Lighting the Whole House: How Many Lights and Where
          </h2>

          <p>
            One lantern is not an outage plan for a family. People scatter: someone is reading in the bedroom, someone is in the bathroom, and someone is looking for the cooler in the garage. A practical setup is one bright lantern for the main living space, one small light per bedroom, one light that stays permanently in the bathroom, and a headlamp or flashlight for every adult. Small battery operated lights are cheap enough that buying a set beats fighting over one good lantern on the second night.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement changes how much light you need. A lantern on the floor lights the floor; the same lantern hung from a hook or set on top of a refrigerator or bookshelf lights the room. Many lanterns, including the Moji and the LuminAID, are designed to hang, and a few cheap ceiling hooks or carabiners installed in advance make a dramatic difference. Bouncing light off a white wall or ceiling spreads it further and cuts glare, which lets you run a lower setting and stretch runtime.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bathrooms and hallways are where outage injuries tend to happen, because people move around them in the dark at night. Stick-on battery motion lights or plug-in night lights with a battery backup handle this nicely without anyone having to remember to switch them on. They do not need to be bright; even a few dozen lumens at ankle height is enough to see a step or a sleeping dog. Kids also cope much better with a small light of their own than with a flashlight they are told not to waste.
          </p>

          <p>
            Light is only one part of keeping a house livable without power. In summer, a <a href="/best-battery-powered-fan/">battery-powered fan</a> often matters as much as a lantern for getting through the night, and in winter the conversation shifts to staying warm safely, which our guide to the <a href="/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/">best indoor heaters for power outages</a> covers in detail. For outages that last more than a day or two, the larger question is backup power for the whole house, the subject of our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide for power outages</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fire-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Candles, Fuel Lanterns and Carbon Monoxide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Candles feel like the natural outage light, and they are one of the worst choices. Fire safety organizations, including the National Fire Protection Association and the U.S. Fire Administration, consistently warn that candle fires spike during power outages, when people place lit candles near curtains, leave them burning in empty rooms, or fall asleep beside them. If you must use a candle, put it in a sturdy holder on a stable, uncluttered surface, keep it well away from anything that burns, and never leave it unattended. A cheap LED lantern makes that risk unnecessary.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fuel-burning lanterns are a different and more serious hazard indoors. Propane, white-gas and kerosene lanterns produce carbon monoxide, a colorless, odorless gas that can build to dangerous levels in a closed room. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention warns against using generators, grills, camp stoves and similar fuel-burning devices inside a home, garage or basement, and the same logic applies to fuel lanterns. They are designed for open-air campsites, not living rooms, and they should never be your primary indoor outage light.
          </p>

          <p>
            If anyone in the house uses fuel-burning equipment during an outage, whether a generator outside or a fuel heater rated for indoor use, make sure you have working carbon monoxide alarms with fresh batteries on every level and near sleeping areas. A hardwired alarm with no battery backup will not protect you when the power is out, which is easy to forget. Battery-only CO alarms are inexpensive and a sensible addition to any outage kit, alongside the lanterns.
          </p>

          <p>
            The good news is that LED lanterns remove almost all of this risk. They run cool, they will not ignite a curtain if knocked over, they produce no fumes, and they are safe to leave on in a child's room overnight. Given how cheap a reasonable LED lantern now is, there is simply no reason to make a flame your main light in an outage. Keep candles and fuel lanterns, if you have them at all, as a last resort with the caveats above.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No fuel lanterns indoors. Candles only with constant supervision.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">LED lanterns are the primary indoor light. Fuel lanterns produce carbon monoxide and belong outdoors. If any fuel-burning equipment runs during an outage, battery-powered CO alarms should be on every level of the home.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Storing Emergency Lights So They Work When Needed
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common emergency-light failure is not a bad lantern. It is a good lantern that was put away dead, or a battery lantern whose alkaline cells leaked and corroded the contacts. Both are entirely preventable with a simple routine. Pick a recurring date, such as when the clocks change for daylight saving time, and use it to charge every rechargeable light, test every battery light, and check the expiry dates on spare cells. It takes ten minutes twice a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store disposable batteries outside the light, in their original packaging or a battery case, and tape a spare set to the lantern or keep them in the same bin. That eliminates leak damage and still keeps the batteries where you will find them. If you prefer lights that are ready instantly, lithium AA and AAA cells are the safer choice to leave installed, since they are much less prone to leaking than alkaline. Never mix old and new cells or different chemistries in one light.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rechargeable lanterns with lithium batteries prefer a cool, dry place, not a hot attic, car trunk or damp basement. Heat ages lithium cells faster than anything else. Lithium batteries also lose a little charge each month in storage, so a lantern charged in spring may be noticeably lower by fall. For an emergency light, the right compromise is to keep it close to full and top it up at each check, rather than storing it at the partial charge that is ideal for long-term battery health.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, store the lights where you can find them in the dark. A lantern in a labeled bin in the basement is not much use if the outage hits at night and the basement stairs are the hazard. Keep at least one light in a known spot on every floor: a kitchen drawer, a bedside table, a hook by the back door. Glow-in-the-dark tape on the handle or the drawer front is a cheap trick that helps. Then make sure everyone in the house knows where those spots are.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which One to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most households, the best emergency lantern is the <a href="#goal-zero-lighthouse-600">Goal Zero Lighthouse 600</a>. It is bright enough to light a main room, has a dimmer to stretch runtime, charges over USB, adds a hand crank as a last resort, and can top up a phone. If you prefer softer, warmer light for evenings and do not need a crank, the <a href="#biolite-alpenglow-500">BioLite AlpenGlow 500</a> is the more pleasant lantern to live with. Either one should be the light that comes out first.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a backup that can sit in a closet for years, buy the <a href="#coleman-400-lumen-led-lantern">Coleman 400 Lumen LED Lantern</a> and store a set of D cells next to it. For a grid-free option that packs flat into a go-bag or car kit, the <a href="#luminaid-packlite-max">LuminAID PackLite Max 2-in-1</a> recharges itself in the sun and floats if things get wet. Neither is as nice to use as the rechargeable lanterns, and both are better at sitting forgotten until needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            For bedrooms and kids, the <a href="#goal-zero-lighthouse-micro-flash">Goal Zero Lighthouse Micro Flash</a> and the <a href="#black-diamond-moji">Black Diamond Moji</a> are small, inexpensive, and easy to hang. Buy one per person and stop the arguments. And every adult should have a headlamp: the <a href="#black-diamond-spot-400-headlamp">Black Diamond Spot 400</a> is a proven, waterproof choice with a red mode that preserves night vision. If you only have a small budget, prioritize one rechargeable lantern and one headlamp, then add the rest over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            What we would skip: no-name "1,000-lumen" lanterns with no brand history and no FL1 rating, solar-only lights with tiny panels that never fully charge, and any lantern that charges through a proprietary plug. A good lantern is cheap enough that there is no reason to gamble on one. If your plans extend past lighting to keeping a fridge, a CPAP or a router running, start with our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station guide</a>, since a station can recharge every light here many times over.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on phone flashlights</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A phone flashlight drains the one device you need for news, alerts and calls, and it lights almost nothing once you set it down. Use phones for communication and buy real lanterns for light. Even a cheap LED lantern on a table beats every phone in the house used as a torch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing alkaline batteries inside the light</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Alkaline cells left in a lantern for a year or more can leak potassium hydroxide, corrode the contacts and ruin the light. Store spares in their packaging beside the lantern, or use lithium AA and AAA cells if the light must be ready instantly. Check everything twice a year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on the biggest lumen number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,000-lumen claim on a no-name lantern tells you little about real runtime or light quality. You will run most lanterns at a third of maximum. Prioritize a good dimmer, a diffused globe, a credible runtime figure and a brand with a track record over a big number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using fuel lanterns or candles indoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Propane, white-gas and kerosene lanterns produce carbon monoxide and should never be your indoor light. Candles cause house fires during outages every year, often in rooms nobody was watching. Use LED lanterns indoors, keep any flame supervised, and install battery-powered CO alarms if fuel equipment runs anywhere near the house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Owning only one light</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One lantern for a whole family means arguments and people walking around in the dark. Plan for a main-room lantern, a small light per bedroom, a bathroom light and a headlamp for each adult. Small battery operated lights are cheap; a twisted ankle on dark stairs is not.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

