        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            An emergency lantern is the cheapest piece of outage gear that makes the biggest difference to how a dark house feels. Buy a rechargeable lantern with a dimmer and USB charging for the main room, a battery lantern that can wait in a closet for years, and a headlamp for every adult.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then do the part most people skip: charge and test everything twice a year, keep spare batteries outside the lights, and never make a candle or a fuel lantern your primary indoor light. A ten-minute routine is the difference between a lantern that works and one that leaked in the drawer.
          </p>
